  <section id="chart" aria-labelledby="chart-h">
    <h2 id="chart-h">How Much Protein Do You Need?</h2>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/protein-per-day-by-goal.webp" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Daily protein in grams for a 150 lb person by goal, from the RDA to fat loss"><figcaption>Daily protein ranges for a 150 lb (68 kg) person.</figcaption></figure><div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Daily protein by goal</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">g per kg</th><th scope="col">g per lb</th><th scope="col">150 lb person</th><th scope="col">200 lb person</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Sedentary (RDA)</th><td>0.8&ndash;1.0</td><td>0.36&ndash;0.45</td><td>54&ndash;68 g</td><td>73&ndash;91 g</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Regular exercise</th><td>1.2&ndash;1.6</td><td>0.54&ndash;0.73</td><td>82&ndash;109 g</td><td>109&ndash;145 g</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Building muscle</th><td>1.6&ndash;2.2</td><td>0.73&ndash;1.00</td><td>109&ndash;150 g</td><td>145&ndash;200 g</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Losing fat</th><td>1.8&ndash;2.4</td><td>0.82&ndash;1.09</td><td>122&ndash;163 g</td><td>163&ndash;218 g</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Adults 65+</th><td>1.0&ndash;1.2</td><td>0.45&ndash;0.54</td><td>68&ndash;82 g</td><td>91&ndash;109 g</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="per-meal" aria-labelledby="per-meal-h">
    <h2 id="per-meal-h">Protein per Meal</h2>
    <p>Muscle protein synthesis is stimulated best by about 0.3&ndash;0.4&nbsp;g of protein per kg per meal, roughly 20&ndash;40&nbsp;g for most adults, spread over 3&ndash;5 meals. Older adults need the higher end per meal. A protein-rich meal within a few hours of training is enough; the exact timing matters little.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="foods" aria-labelledby="foods-h">
    <h2 id="foods-h">High-Protein Foods</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table"><caption>About 25 g of protein</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Food</th><th scope="col">Amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Chicken breast, cooked</th><td>85&ndash;100 g (3&ndash;3.5 oz)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Canned tuna</th><td>1 can (about 110 g drained)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Eggs</th><td>4 large</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Greek yogurt</th><td>250 g (1 cup)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lentils, cooked</th><td>280 g (1&frac12; cups)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tofu, firm</th><td>170 g (6 oz)</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Whey protein</th><td>1 scoop</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </section>

  <section id="safety" aria-labelledby="safety-h">
    <h2 id="safety-h">Is Too Much Protein Harmful?</h2>
    <p>For healthy people, intakes up to about 2.2&ndash;2.5&nbsp;g/kg have not been shown to harm the kidneys or bones. People with kidney disease should follow their doctor&rsquo;s advice. Very high protein can crowd out fiber-rich foods, so keep an eye on the <a href="/fiber-calculator/">fiber calculator</a> too, and plan the whole day with the <a href="/macro-calculator/">macro calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Protein FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How much protein do I need a day?</h3></summary><p>At least 0.8 g per kg of body weight (0.36 g per lb). If you exercise, 1.2–1.6 g/kg; to build muscle, 1.6–2.2 g/kg.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How much protein to build muscle?</h3></summary><p>About 1.6 g per kg of body weight per day captures most of the benefit; up to 2.2 g/kg may help some people, especially when losing fat.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is 1 gram of protein per pound too much?</h3></summary><p>It is on the high side but safe for healthy people. Research suggests about 0.7 g per lb (1.6 g/kg) is enough for most lifters.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How much protein can you absorb in one meal?</h3></summary><p>You absorb almost all the protein you eat. For muscle building, 20–40 g per meal is a good target, but larger meals are not wasted.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Do older adults need more protein?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Experts recommend 1.0–1.2 g/kg for adults over 65 to help preserve muscle, more with exercise or illness.</p></details>
  </section>

